    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d697">

                <pb facs="0594" n="582" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg582"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890F.6363/38: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-08-20T21:00:00+03:00">August 20, 1942—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 20—4:27 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1466. The substance of Department’s 772, July 20, 10 p.m. was brought to
                    attention of General Spalding upon his arrival in Cairo and prior to his
                    departure for Iran and Iraq. He has now returned to Cairo en route to Washington
                    and informs me that owing to his other duties it was impossible for him to
                    investigate denial operations and plans affecting Casoc oil fields.</p>
                <pb facs="0595" n="583" xml:id="pg583"/>
                <p>I have shown him telegram No. 47 from AmLegation Jedda and he states that he sees
                    no reason why Casoc should not facilitate preparation of plan for denial
                    operation without regard to matter of possible active preparation for such
                    operations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
